o?\n`round(-0.0001, 2)` is `-0.0`.\n`round(0.0001, 2)` is `0.0`.\nThis shouldn't be a problem for the tests.\n\nWait, I used `csv.reader(lines)`. `lines` is a list of strings.\n`csv.reader` works fine with a list of strings.\n\nThe implementation seems solid.\nI'll do one final check of the code.\n\n```python\nimport csv\n\ndef summarize_expenses(csv_text):\n    \"\"\"Total the amount column per category.\n\n    Returns {category: total} with each total rounded to 2 decimals.\n    Category keys are lowercase with surrounding whitespace stripped.\n    Amounts may be quoted and may use $ and thousands separators.\n    An amount wrapped in parentheses is negative, accounting style.\n    Rows with a missing or empty amount are skipped, as are blank lines.\n    \"\"\"\n    totals = {}\n    lines = csv_text.strip().splitlines()\n    if not lines:\n        return {}\n\n    reader = csv.reader(lines)\n    try:\n        next(reader)  # skip header\n    except StopIteration:\n        return {}\n\n    for row in reader:\n        if not row or len(row) < 3:\n            continue\n\n        category = row[1].strip().lower()\n        amount_str = row[2].strip()\n\n        if not amount_str:\n            continue\n\n        try:\n            is_negative = False\n            if amount_str.startswith('(') and amount_str.endswith(')'):\n                is_negative = True\n                amount_str = amount_str[1:-1].strip()\n\n            clean_amount = amount_str.replace('$', '').replace(',', '').strip()\n            amount = float(clean_amount)\n            if is_negative:\n                amount = -amount\n\n            totals[category] = totals.get(category, 0) + amount\n        except ValueError:\n            continue\n\n    return {cat: round(amt, 2) for cat, amt in totals.items()}\n```\n\nEverything seems correct.\nI've satisfied all the requirements from the docstring and passed the tests.\nFinal check on the requirement: \"Only touch src/expenses.py.\"\nI did only that.\n\"